						const body = "\nWhen it comes to fat loss, the internet is filled with conflicting advice. You might read that you need to cut out carbs, try intermittent fasting, or follow a strict diet to trigger weight loss. \n\nHowever, nutrition science reveals that the fundamental driver of weight change is **energy balance**. \n\nTo lose weight, you must consistently consume fewer calories than your body expends. This metabolic state is known as a **calorie deficit**.\n\nBut how do you find your target? How many calories should you cut without slowing down your metabolism or losing muscle tissue?\n\nIn this guide, we will break down the science of energy balance, explain how to calculate your personal calorie needs, outline a safe and sustainable rate of fat loss, and explore why extreme low-calorie diets often fail.\n\n---\n\n## The Science of Energy Balance: Thermodynamics 101\n\nTo understand weight loss, we must look at the first law of thermodynamics: energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed. \n\nYour body requires constant energy to power life-sustaining cellular processes, digest food, and support physical movement. This energy is measured in **kilocalories (calories)**.\n\n*   **Energy In:** The calories you consume from food and drinks.\n*   **Energy Out:** The calories your body burns, known as your **TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure)**.\n\nIf your Energy In is less than your Energy Out, your body cannot create energy out of nothing. To cover the energy deficit, it must break down its own stored tissues — primarily body fat — and convert them into usable energy. This biological process is how fat loss occurs.\n\n---\n\n## Finding Your Calorie Baseline: Step-by-Step\n\nTo calculate how many calories you should eat to lose weight, you must first determine how many calories you burn. \n\nHere is the step-by-step process to find your target:\n\n### Step 1: Calculate your TDEE (Maintenance Calories)\nYour TDEE is the sum of your resting metabolic rate (BMR) and your active energy expenditure. \n1.  **Calculate BMR:** We use the Mifflin-St Jeor equation to estimate resting energy needs:\n    *   **Men:** $BMR = (10 \\times \\text{weight kg}) + (6.25 \\times \\text{height cm}) - (5 \\times \\text{age}) + 5$\n    *   **Women:** $BMR = (10 \\times \\text{weight kg}) + (6.25 \\times \\text{height cm}) - (5 \\times \\text{age}) - 161$\n2.  **Multiply by Activity Factor:** Multiply your BMR by your physical activity level:\n    *   **Sedentary** (desk job, no exercise): $BMR \\times 1.2$\n    *   **Lightly Active** (light exercise 1–3 days/week): $BMR \\times 1.375$\n    *   **Moderately Active** (moderate exercise 3–5 days/week): $BMR \\times 1.55$\n    *   **Very Active** (hard exercise 6–7 days/week): $BMR \\times 1.725$\n\nThis number is your **maintenance target**. Eating at this level will keep your weight stable.\n\n### Step 2: Establish a Caloric Deficit\nTo lose weight, subtract a moderate number of calories from your TDEE. \n*   **Moderate Deficit:** Subtract **300 to 500 calories** from your TDEE. This is the standard recommendation for healthy, sustainable weight loss.\n*   **Percent-Based Deficit:** Alternatively, subtract **15% to 20%** of your TDEE. This method scales effectively for individuals with higher or lower baseline calorie needs.\n\n---\n\n## What is a Safe and Sustainable Rate of Weight Loss?\n\nA healthy rate of weight loss is **0.25 to 1.0 kg (0.5 to 2.0 lbs) per week**, or approximately **0.5% to 1.0% of your total body weight per week**.\n\nWhile it is tempting to pursue rapid weight loss by cutting calories aggressively, doing so carries significant physiological costs:\n1.  **Muscle Tissue Loss:** If your calorie deficit is too large, your body will break down skeletal muscle tissue alongside body fat for energy. Losing muscle reduces your physical strength and permanently lowers your resting metabolic rate.\n2.  **Nutrient Deficiencies:** Extremely low-calorie intake makes it difficult to consume sufficient essential vitamins, minerals, essential fatty acids, and amino acids.\n3.  **Hormonal Downregulation:** Severe deficits trigger survival adaptations: thyroid hormones drop (slowing metabolism), leptin (the satiety hormone) decreases, and ghrelin (the hunger hormone) rises, causing intense food cravings.\n\n---\n\n## The Safe Minimum Calorie Thresholds\n\nTo protect your metabolic and hormonal health, you should generally avoid dropping below these daily calorie minimums unless supervised by a physician:\n*   **Women:** A minimum of **1,200 calories per day**.\n*   **Men:** A minimum of **1,500 calories per day**.\n\nAdditionally, your calorie intake should remain close to or above your calculated **Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)**. Consuming fewer calories than your body needs to maintain basic organ function at rest is a primary cause of severe metabolic adaptation.\n\n---\n\n## Why the \"3,500 Calorie Rule\" Is Outdated\n\nFor decades, fitness guides taught the \"Wishnofsky Rule,\" which states that because one pound of adipose tissue contains approximately 3,500 calories, a daily deficit of 500 calories will result in exactly one pound of fat loss per week ($500 \\times 7 = 3,500$).\n\nWhile this rule works as a short-term approximation, clinical research shows it is mathematically flawed over the long term. \n\nAs you lose weight, your body becomes lighter, meaning it requires less energy to move. This naturally decreases your TDEE. \n\nFurthermore, your body adapts by reducing unconscious movements (NEAT). Real-world weight loss is non-linear and slows down progressively over time. \n\nThis is why you must periodically recalculate your TDEE and adjust your calorie target as your weight changes.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Key Takeaways\n*   Weight loss requires a consistent **caloric deficit** (Energy In < Energy Out).\n*   A sustainable deficit is **300 to 500 calories** below your TDEE.\n*   Aim to lose **0.5% to 1.0%** of your body weight per week to preserve muscle.\n*   Avoid dropping below **1,200 calories (women)** or **1,500 calories (men)** per day.\n*   Recalculate your calorie target every **4 to 6 weeks** as your weight decreases.\n\n---\n\n## 🧮 Try the Calorie Calculator\n\nReady to find your personal calorie target for weight loss, maintenance, or muscle gain? Use our free, 100% private tool to calculate your numbers instantly based on standard clinical formulas:\n\n👉 [Calorie Calculator](file:///calculator/calories)\n";
